# Design docs

## Locations

| Document | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `docs/design.ja.md` | Canonical design (Japanese). Requirements, threat model, formats, protocols, contracts, phases |
| `docs/design-decisions.md` | Rejected alternatives and why (Decision / Reason / Rejected) |
| `docs/adr/NNNN-*.md` | One decision per file whose reason is a measurement or an outside fact (Status, Date, Issue, Context, Decision, Consequences) |
| `docs/roadmap.md` | Rows: ID, title, Issue, status. No acceptance text |
| `docs/conventions.md` | Directory tree, project responsibilities, coding conventions |

`.dev/` is local scratch and is gitignored. Do not put lasting decisions there.

## Changing the design

- Requirement, contract, public API, wire or file format, threat model, or phase: open a `design` Issue, update `docs/design.ja.md` (and `docs/design-decisions.md` for what was rejected) in its PR, and merge it before implementation Issues.
- Exception for a row split by a Grill: the detail of one row that its own Grill decided (endpoints, formats, the order of checks, the client API of that row) may ship in that row's implementation PR, after an independent design review of the diff before the PR is opened. Requirements, the threat model, and phases still go first through a design PR. The owner decided this for M4 (decision 0.61, Issue #38) after M3 had worked that way.
- A threat-model PR that goes ahead of its row may name settings, defaults, and endpoints that the row's design has not written yet. The row's implementation PR then checks, under Verification, that every name and default §5 uses matches §14 and §15 as merged. In M5b the threat model named `session.idleMinutes`, its 30 minutes, and `POST /auth/logout` before §15.3 had them (PR #111's review).
- A Grill answer that the owner overturns after the design review goes into `docs/design-decisions.md` (Decision / Reason / Rejected) in the row's PR, with the rest of that row's design. M5d1 and M5d2 left four such answers out, and the review of PR #130 asked for them; F1 left out the overturned SBOM answer (Q15), and the review of PR #154 asked for it. Before calling the PR ready, list every Grill answer the row changed and find each in that file.
- Wording, typos, and examples: may ship in the same PR as code.
- Do not implement first and update the design later. If code and design conflict, stop and fix the design through the process above.

## What a design statement must carry

- A claim about what another system or standard does (FlexNet, Keygen, JWS, an OS API) names the document page, RFC section, or source file and line where it was read, and is read there before it goes in. A rule taken from a specification (an RFC, a NIST publication) is copied from the section, read for the purpose, and the section is named. The M6 split said that an exception leaving an `[UnmanagedCallersOnly]` function ends the process; the owner's review asked for the source, and none of the four pages read said so (PR #134). The first threat rows of the release path named what GitHub, NuGet, and macOS do without a page; the independent review found most of its 16 findings there, among them that `gh attestation verify --repo` does not check the signing workflow, that nuget.org adds its own signature, and that a tag run restores the default branch's cache (PR #151). An input of a GitHub Action is read in the `action.yml` of the pinned commit before the design gives it a reason: F1 set `create-storage-record: false` to drop a permission, and the input does nothing unless `push-to-registry` is on (PR #154).
- A mitigation is checked, one by one, against every decision of the Grill that touches it. In the release threats T22 gave the publishing permissions to the approved job only, while the Grill had the draft release and the attestation made before the approval (PR #151).
- A design that depends on a library's fine behavior (which value it protects, when it reloads, which exception reaches which event) reads the source of the branch of the version in use, not only the documentation, and names the file. The first M5b design said Data Protection protected the content of the correlation and nonce cookies; both values are `N`, and what is protected is the `state` and the nonce cookie's name. It also said Data Protection does not reload on an unknown key, because the documentation said nothing; the release/10.0 source reloads during the first 2 minutes (Issue #109, second review, R2 and R6). The same design asked the OpenIdConnect handler's validator for `RequireStateValidation = true`; the handler checks the state itself and gives the validator none, so every callback was refused. Its default `ClaimActions` remove `iss` after the token is checked, which the callback read. Both showed only in the implementation (PR #114). A handler option goes into the design after its use in the handler's source has been read.
- A statement of what this repository's own code does (when a row is written, which check runs first) is read in that code before it goes in, and names the method. Decision 0.102 said that each read of the audit log put its own `audit.list` row at the top; `AdminAsync` writes the row after the answer, so it heads the next read, and only the headless Chrome check showed it (PR #126).
- A format (license token, request, response) gives every field: its type, its encoding, whether it is signed, whether it is required, and what a reader does with an unknown field or an unknown version.
- A protocol gives every message, every error code, the order of the checks, and what each side does on a timeout, a retry, and a duplicate.
- A table of endpoints comes with, for each endpoint, the request body (each member's type, whether it is required, its limits, and what is done with an unknown member), the answer's shape, the error codes (a code `§19` lacks is added in the same PR), and the rate limit. In PR #43 a table of paths and permissions went out alone, and the review asked for the rest.
- A value that goes into a hash or a signature is returned by an API as the stored bytes, or the design writes the normalization that makes the bytes again. In PR #43 the audit log's `detail` was to be returned as a parsed object, from which a reader could not make the hashed string again.
- A design that reads input from outside (a token file, a request body, a key file, an OS value) treats it as untrusted and writes its limits (size, depth, count) and what happens past them. A size the input claims is checked against what the input can hold before a buffer of that size is made.
- A security statement names the threat (who the attacker is, what they hold, what they want) and the property that stops it. A threat the design accepts is written as accepted, with the reason.
- A design that adds threads, timers, or OS resources writes their lifetime: who creates them, when they stop, who releases them, and what happens when they cannot be created.
- A design that uses a value read from the OS (a machine ID, the clock) says what it is read for, when it is read, when it is read again, and what happens when it cannot be read.
- A design that leaves normalizing or parsing to a BCL type writes what that type does, read from its documentation or tried in code, not what the design would like it to do. A test fixes the edge cases. In M3a, §12.1 said the path was otherwise left as it is, while `System.Uri` decodes unreserved characters and removes `.` and `..` segments (RFC 3986 §6.2.2); the owner's review found it (PR #16).
- A statement that keeps a record longer (a request's `jti` kept until its answer is saved) writes how the record grows when the same failure repeats, and what happens at its limit. In M4b2 the fix from the first review kept one `jti` for each hourly retry of a file the client could not keep, and the list of 16 filled in 16 hours; the second review found it (PR #67).
- A design that keeps only the latest record of something that also lives outside the service (a request file people carry, a copy on a USB stick) writes what an older copy does when it arrives after a newer one. In M4c1 the service kept one answer an activation, and an old request file passed after a new one was judged a clone; the review found it, and it became an accepted threat (PR #71).
- A design that returns something again later (a waiting request made again) and keeps a copy of state taken when it was made (the list of unanswered requests) writes how that copy goes stale and what happens then. In M4c2 the client returned a waiting request whose retry list no longer named an online request whose answer was lost after it; the review found it (PR #75).
- A design whose new owner shares a file with an existing one (a state file, a key folder) compares how each writes it: the format, the signature, what each keeps in memory. In M4c3 the server's clock guard wrote its state unsigned while the ActivationClient that shared the file signed it; M4b2 had fixed the same gap in LicenseClient and missed the server (PR #79).
- A figure the design derives (a token size, a lease count, a memory bound) writes its arithmetic beside it.
- A guard that rests on a setting of an outside tool (a package manager, a compiler, a CI service) writes the setting explicitly instead of relying on its default, and the PR shows the guard refusing once. In W0 the design relied on pnpm's default `minimumReleaseAge`, which is not strict and installs a version that is too new; the review found it, and an install of a 17-hour-old package was refused only after the setting was written (PR #95, R3). The owner's review asked the same for `strictDepBuilds`.
- Every type in a signature the design writes is defined in the design or the code.
- A design that leaves a point to another row ("row X decides") names a row that exists in `docs/roadmap.md`, and that row's text names the point, in the same PR (`.cursor/rules/defer.mdc`).
- A design PR that splits a row lists, for each new row, the points its Grill decides: what the design leaves open in the sections that row touches. The roadmap row points at that list. A row's title and its list of contents are not enough. In the M5 split, about ten of the independent review's 18 findings were open points no row named (how the CLI's last login is counted, where the login is stored, permissions, rate limits, the Data Protection keys); §14.6 got the list (PR #99).
- A design PR that changes a decision searches the whole design for the sections that restate it, and changes them in the same PR. Search by the decision's number (`0.xx`) and by its key words. In D0, decision 0.39 moved the certificate check to the token's `iat`, and §7.3 and T12 kept the old current-time rule until the owner's review found them (PR #4). The search covers `.cursor/rules/` and the skills too: in PR #39, decision 0.61 let a row's detail ship in its implementation PR while `design-docs.mdc` and `workflow.mdc` still said otherwise, and the review found it.
- A decision that widens a restriction also changes the decisions and the accepted threats (§5.4) that state the restriction. Search by the restriction's words as well as by numbers. In M4a2, decision 0.86 let `elm-service` issue a token without expiry, while 0.17 and §5.4 still said such tokens came from the CLI only (PR #51).
- New decision rows go after the last row of the table, in number order. In M4a2 the rows 0.82 to 0.87 went in before 0.81 (PR #51).
- A state kept partly as stored marks and partly computed (a stored suspension, an expiry computed from the clock) writes the order in which overlapping states resolve, and the state diagram shows every transition that order allows. In M4a2 a suspended license whose expiry had passed had no state until the review asked (PR #51).
- A constraint added for a combination of settings is checked against what each existing constraint assumes. In M4a2 the trial rule assumed fingerprints, and the new `unbound` binding has none (PR #51).
- A create request does not carry a reference that cannot exist yet at creation. In M4a2 a product's trial policy names a policy of that product, so it is set by `PATCH` only (PR #51).
- A statement that is an exception to an existing "every X" statement (every operation is audited) either drops the exception or writes it into the "every X" statement (PR #51).

After a script edits a Markdown table, read `git diff --stat`. A diff of the whole table means its columns were padded again: restore the file and replace only the rows that change.

## Review before the PR

A design PR, and a feature PR that adds or changes statements of the design, is reviewed by an independent reader before it is opened: an agent that reads only the documents and the rules, not the conversation that wrote them, and reports security holes, contradictions, dangling references, and rule violations. Each finding is fixed in the design or put to the owner as a question. The review's summary goes on the Issue as a comment, so that the PR body can cite its finding IDs. In D0 this review found 35 points in the first draft, among them a fake server minting leases from a trial license and a negative seat count (Issue #3). In M3b the review of the design added by the feature PR found that two copies of a cloned VM, renewing at the same moment, passed the D0 rule "one sequence number behind is a retry" and could share one seat (PR #22, decision 0.47).

## Judge the current optimum

The design records a decision made at that time. It is not proof that the decision is still the best one.

- Recommend the best solution for the problem as it stands now.
- Do not cite an existing sentence as the reason to keep that shape.
- Do not add public API only so an older shape can stay.
- If the written decision is no longer the best one, say so and change the design through the process above.
